## 7. Blocking findings (round 1)

### B1 — the PR lands the misfiled W-028 evidence it was created to retire

$ git ls-tree master --name-only studio/ci/reds/W-028/
    17.log 18.log 19.log 21.log check-real-officina.log close-bugs-*.log
    review-tier-*.log
$ git ls-tree pr3-head --name-only studio/ci/reds/W-028/
    … + 22.log 23.log 24.log
$ git log --oneline master..pr3-head -- studio/ci/reds/W-028/
    06f10aa feat(context): point every sella at the CLI usage banner

These three files are NOT on master. They are PR #1's reds, carried in on
the first of this PR's two commits and never removed by the second. Merging
#3 writes them into the trunk for the first time.

They are precisely the artefact this PR's own body calls a mistake:
  - filed under behaviours 22-24, which briefs/W-028.md does not declare
    (it declares 1-5, all `checkpointStale`) — so they can never satisfy
    `opus.red_evidence`, which still blocks W-028 on "missing red log(s)
    for behaviour(s) 1, 2, 3, 4, 5";
  - `# sella: guest` — unattributable, round 3's A2, uncorrected;
  - 22.log's mutation is misdescribed in PR #1's body (round 3's A1);
  - and they are strictly redundant: reds/W-030/01-04 are the same
    mutations against the same assertions, correctly numbered and
    attributed.

W-028 is an open opus. Landing three reds for behaviours it does not have,
in the PR whose stated premise is "that numbering was wrong, this is the
opus it belongs to", is the officina's recurring finding class
(review×evidence-backfilled-or-absent, 4 cascades) in its purest form: the
code is fine and the evidence does not say what it claims to say.

Fix: `git rm studio/ci/reds/W-028/{22,23,24}.log`. No source change, no
gate re-run. One command.

NOTE FOR THE ORCHESTRATOR: the review task I was given asserts these files
"still exist on master and should eventually be removed". That is factually
wrong in a way that matters — a merge performed on that belief lands them
believing it is a no-op. They exist only on the branch.

### B2 — W-030 has no probationes at all, and never entered the lifecycle

$ cat studio/opera/W-030.md
    state: backlog
    probationes: {}

There is no `tests`, `lint`, `types` or `spec` certificate on this opus. I
re-ran all three myself (§1) and they are green, so the facts are
established — but a fact I establish in a review log is not a probatio, and
a claim in a PR body is not one either. That was round 3's B2 wording and it
applies unchanged; absent certificates are strictly worse than the stale
ones I failed #1 for.

The opus is also `backlog` while its work is complete and under review, so
`opus.red_evidence` never evaluates against it and the six conforming reds
in §5 are checked by nothing.

This is the orchestrator's step, not the builder's, and it is why this is a
merge precondition rather than a defect in the work: `greenlight` / `ready`
/ `verify W-030 --studio studio --repo .` against the tree that merges, then
`done`. It must happen before the merge, not after — `verify` refuses a
dirty tree and a certificate recorded post-merge names a different tree.

## 8. Advisory findings

A1. The drift guard silently skips what it cannot parse. M5/M6 above: a
    constant rewritten as a template literal, or given a `: string`
    annotation, is not matched by `extractUsageConstants`' regex and
    disappears from the guard along with any drift in it — no failure, no
    warning. `sawAny` only requires that ONE constant was found, so 29 of
    the 30 could vanish silently. A pinned count closes it in one line:
    `check("scanned every known usage constant", found === 30)`. Cheap, and
    it fails loudly the day the count legitimately changes, which is exactly
    when someone should look.

A2. Two undeclared paths. `packages/commands/src/index.ts` (+1 line) is in
    the diff and in no list. And the three files the amendment adds
    (pause/serve/tick) are named in the brief's PROSE, not in its "Files
    owned" bullets — so a future `probatio.declared` check, parsing the
    bullet list as every other consumer does, would flag them anyway. Move
    them into bullets and add index.ts.

A3. The guard is one-directional and four banner lines have no backing
    constant: `check`, `init`, `context`, `query` — exactly the four
    commands whose flags live in main.ts's `FLAGS_BY_COMMAND` rather than a
    `*_USAGE` string. Their banner lines are correct today (I compared them
    against the table by hand) but nothing keeps them so. The brief puts
    "reconciling USAGE against the parseFlags allowlists" out of scope, so
    this is a limit of the delivered feature rather than a defect in it —
    worth naming in the brief's out-of-scope section so the next reader
    knows the guard's coverage is 30 of 34 lines, not 34 of 34.

A4. Behaviour 6's red covers three of its four paths. reds/W-030/06.log
    shows `unknown flag: prints banner, exits 2` PASSING under the stated
    mutation (main.ts's fallback muted) — that path reaches the banner
    through a different call site, which the log itself proves, since the
    pre-existing `unknown flag exits 2` assertion in the same run prints the
    full banner to stderr. The behaviour as a whole does go red, so the red
    conforms; but the fourth assertion has never been demonstrated to fail
    and is the one that would survive a regression in that call site.

A5. All six reds record `tree: dirty:d34e898e…`, which is PR #1's head tree,
    not this branch's. Reds are dirty by construction (a mutation is
    applied) and the prefix is honestly disclosed, so this is not a defect —
    but the base hash names a tree that is not in this PR's history, which
    makes the reds harder to locate in time than they need to be. Producing
    reds from the branch they ship on would cost nothing.

## 9. Not a defect

The design judgement in round 3's §9 stands and I re-verified the part that
this PR changed: `packages/commands/package.json` still declares no
dependency on `@bisellium/cli`, `cli` declares `commands`, and the new
`packages/cli/src/usage.ts` shim follows the W-016 verify.ts/talk.ts pattern
exactly. No cycle.

Replacing "hand-fix the two lines round 3 named" with "assert the class and
let it find the rest" was the right call and it paid immediately: it found
`emit --usage`, which nobody — including me, with 33 grep hits in front of
me in round 3 — had named. That is the correct response to a review finding:
close the class, not the instance.

Behaviour 4's realistic-lex fixture is the right shape for the bug it pins.
It asserts `c.truncated.includes("lex")` alongside the pointer's presence,
so it fails both when the pointer is dropped AND when the lex is small
enough that the case is never exercised — which is exactly how the original
test lied.

## 10. Verdict

FAIL — round 1.

The engineering is done and I could verify every claim it makes. B1 and B2
of round 3 are genuinely closed, proved by execution with a control, not by
reading the diff; both surviving mutants are caught; the six reds are
distinct, assertion-level, attributed and reproducible; the scope amendment
is honest.

What fails is two things around it, neither of which is a code change:

  - three reds for behaviours that do not exist are riding into the trunk
    inside the PR that exists to un-misfile them (B1) — `git rm`;
  - the opus carries no certificate of any kind and is still in `backlog`
    (B2) — greenlight/ready/verify/done before the merge.

Round 3's closing line was that the officina keeps making the same finding:
the code is fine and the evidence does not say what it claims to say. Both
items above are that finding again, smaller each time. They are also both
a single command each, so round 2 should be minutes, not a rebuild.

To pass:
  - remove studio/ci/reds/W-028/{22,23,24}.log from the branch;
  - record tests/lint/types on W-030 against the tree that merges, and move
    the opus out of backlog;
  - (advisory, take or leave) pin the constant count in usage.test.ts,
    move pause/serve/tick into the brief's Files-owned bullets and add
    packages/commands/src/index.ts.
